# REPAIR BRIEF r1 — Book 8, Movement 3 "The Border Road" (ch13–19)

Same author, same method (NO-SOURCE: do not open `books/` or `v3-runs/`). You may read this edition's own closed text and the plan. The four Sol reads are in this folder (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`); read them before you touch a line, but **this brief overrides the plan audit wherever they differ** — the audit proposed 102 line replacements and the coordinator has declined most of them. The baseline is `pre-repair-r1/` (your draft plus the 11 coordinator-applied fixes in `plan-audit-apply.md`). Write `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md` when done and refresh AUTHOR-REPORT's "New canon minted" and "State at movement end".

## A. KEPT — do not change (coordinator rulings against the audit)

1. **Your staging of the cut stands whole** (audit fixes 2–39, 41, 43 declined): the Ember grain at the mark, the den that has learned to run to where a declaration ends, Karis, Oryn and the mule at the foot, Vastin's "I beg your pardon" and the wall two feet from Cael, the burst away to the north bank, the heap, the runnel, Seln in the heap, the three through the ice of the sink, Karis's sixth line, Cael's "I planned it wrong", and the slope as you have it. BOOK_MAP §8 calls the cut's positions "private staging the author owns", and your version makes the protected log ("two feet from me") literally true. **Deployment stays thirty-two.**
2. **Oryn's past stands as you wrote it** (audit fixes 78–85 declined): the house for the sick, the column, the fever week, the woman from the river sheds, the keeper who was right by that house's sums. One change only, for source distance (item C3 below).
3. **Cael lays his hand on the page on the rise** (audit fixes 95–100 declined). No plan line places the first touch anywhere else.
4. **The slip stays as the packet gives it** (audit fix 1 declined).
5. **The field mark** "on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk" is right (locked Book 7 ch58: "Thirty-four. Thirty-four. Every one."), and so is Karis's "two of our places, in the field, and not on the others". Audit fixes 61–62 and the editorial's item 2 are declined.
6. The factor without a pronoun; Vastin's right knee and "eleven"; the crossbow north with Oryn; the rope refused; the purse at 138; the Seln cutaway's length.

## B. Already applied by the coordinator (keep; smooth the joins)

7. The six markers are filled: ch16 `"It's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe," said Seln.`; ch16 `"You do now," Cael said.`; ch17 `Karis said, "Two copies of one ignorance."`; ch18 `"Treswick," Vastin said.` / `Karis said, "The archive under it. Cleon."` / `"Who's Cleon?" Cael said.`; ch19 `"That's on me," said Oryn. "It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it."`; ch19 `"Not the steps," she said. "Not the square."` Check each join: who has just spoken, where Seln is standing when he answers Brom, Vastin saying the town's name first, and the paragraph after the Cleon exchange.
8. **The loads at the fork (ch19), to meet Movement 4's opening:** the three boxes are on Brom (his good shoulder), Lira (both arms) and Cael; Seln carries none; **Vastin goes back to the fork-stone for the tarred kit case and takes it up unasked**, after Oryn has gone. The applied lines are plain; re-compose them in your voice, make every later mention agree (Lira sets down a box on the rise, not the case), and re-write the closing file down off the rise: **Vastin walks beside Cael from the rise onward**, not last (Movement 4 opens with "Vastin has walked beside Cael since the rise"). Nobody asks him to, and nobody tells him no. Oryn's "you're not to carry" and his taking the lightest thing when she is out of sight should sit together honestly.

## C. For you to recompose

1. **No number of places, and no fifth place (ch17, ch18:161, ch19:239).** The plan holds the count and the fifth for a later movement (the 298th, when the grade is read: a different mark, and a point past the fourth on the line). So in daylight Karis still gives no number: she can tell one sign-group from another, she can match **four** to the hide by their measures (three alike to the stride and one double), and there are groups she cannot match and will not call places until a sign can be read. Cut "Five", "And one over", the fifth's measure ("larger again than the fourth") and "that square is not filled as ours are", and everything that leans on them (Seln's "the same five places", Vastin's "There are five places", Cael's "five small marks", "five sheets"). Closed chapter 11 has her promise a number "when she has checked it in daylight"; let her answer that promise honestly (the paper gave her groups, not places). Keep "More than four" out of her mouth here — it was printed once, in chapter 11.
2. **No second calendar (ch17:127–133; ch18 cutaway and Cael's sum).** Cut the stations' year that "opens later", the quarrel about founding, the difference that "any child could have subtracted", Karis writing it in her margin, and every later use of it ("taking off", "the turning", "He took off the difference", "A child could have done it"). The series calendar is the owner's, and the sum must owe nothing to Vastin. What stands: the abstracts' first column is the day in the registry's own reckoning; the technician's sheet carries a registry year and a day in it; Seln knew the day when he saw it because he has copied the first line of Cael's file twice; Cael knows it because every paper ever served on him began with it. Seln's doubt for two days is honest without arithmetic (a date read once, over a shoulder, by one lamp, in another person's pencil, by a man who wished it otherwise) and his certainty now is the original in his own hands in daylight, read a figure at a time. Cael's "sum" is a date laid against a date, checked twice. No figures on the page, no span of years, nothing spoken, nothing written. Vastin still explains what the form is (second schedule; the field book called in) and still has his two reflexes there.
3. **Three tracked passages (source-tracking.md): break the beat order, do not reword.** ch17:89 (the finger beside the ruled space, "not on it"); ch18:139 (the first thing he has known and not entered → how easy it was); ch19:33 ("It doesn't matter which; they're all one city…"). The report suggests a different entry for each.
4. **The ruler line, verbatim (ch17:93–103).** The map places it here: Karis stops her own lamps sentence with `A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it.` — her words, exactly, once in this movement (it stood twice in chapter 5). Keep "It was as a row of lamps—" and her refusal to finish.
5. **The surveyor's four words (ch17:77–93, :121; ch18:161).** Karis does not identify the hand: she may lay the dead man's ledger beside the sheet, and she will not swear to a hand on four words, either way. Cut Brom's "And the man who wrote them went down forty steps with a stake, and never came up" and Vastin's "and is dead" — nothing joins that remark to the Stair's dead surveyor. In her gloss of the remark, do not use the word "made" or ask who could have made such a thing (that word is reserved for a later finding); she may say what a device does (the same at every hour, for every person, with an edge that could be ruled) and that he does not say why it cannot be one.
6. **Clarity at the spring (both reviews).** A cold reader loses which discovery matters most between "Then I shall read for what repeats" and the grade. At two or three existing turns, let somebody plain say what has just changed (the measures have not moved in six years; the instruments read true; somebody read the remark and closed the space). Break a paragraph where the question changes. Do not shorten the investigation. In chapter 13's opening, one anchoring noun or clause where a backward reference is essential; no recap.
7. **Oryn's reading owed (ch19:175–179).** Her restored line now follows your lead-in; make sure the lead-in does not say the same thing twice.

