   223	At sixty strides there was a flat stone at the road's edge, and Brom stepped onto it and set his feet, with the rock held against his thigh. Vastin stopped behind him. Cael did not see where Lira went; there was a small dry rattle on the scree above, and then nothing. Seln had left them at the foot, and was already nowhere.
   224	
   225	Brom looked at him, once. Then the big man put his right hand flat, palm down, at his side.
   226	
   227	Cael went on by himself.
   228	
   229	It was twenty strides, and they took him longer than the sixty had. At the eightieth the cut's head stood above him, black and wet, with the seep coming out under it in a skin over the stones. He stooped, and laid a twist of tarred tow on the ice, in the middle, and stepped back two paces onto the turf. His mouth was dry.
   230	
   231	He looked at the tow.
   232	
   233	*Yes*, he told the fifth of the ten, for the first time in earnest since a stair.
   234	
   235	A seed of yellow stood on the tar. It grew for one breath to the size of a barley grain, and that was all he gave it. He shut Ember as he would have shut a hand. The heat that ought to have gone somewhere came home along his left arm instead, though he had been holding nothing, and the old weal across that palm woke and stung as though he had laid it on a kettle.
   236	
   237	He turned his back on the mark, and walked.
   238	
   239	Wind had his feet before the first stride was down. It leaned him at the foot of the cut with everything that it had, and he told it no, and found that his own two legs had something to offer in its place: a walk, a little bent, close under the scree, such as a girl on a bench had beaten into him with the two halves of a count. He did not run. A thing that ran on that road would be looked at.
   240	
   241	He heard them at the tenth stride. It was a sound like gravel poured from a cart, away up on his left, beyond the bank; and it did not come from three places. It came from the whole upper half of the cut together.
   242	
   243	They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
   244	
   245	He was two strides from Brom's stone. He stopped, and stood, and counted eight.
   246	
   247	They reached the tow, where there was a smear of soot on the ice and nothing else. He waited for them to stand.
   248	
   249	They did not so much as check. The first of them set its feet on the glass at the mark, and turned its head down the hill, toward the foot, where three carts lay; and all eight of them came on.

