   299	*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day, night. The first boat brought no paper for us. It brought one man, who was the Archmarshal and is not. He held the ferry on her cable with his back. He has handed over three things: six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet. The town is the one that she named. The price is what it was. I said that I would rule when the boat was in. Tomorrow, at the plank, with hands.*
