editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-13-He stood over it, and Ember made its offer. It was the fifth of the ten things that he carried, and it would set one small hot point on whatever he looked at for long enough; it was looking at the ice now, through his eyes, like a child at a window. He told it no. A point was a thing spent, and what it burned was the hand that carried it. He put his heel through the ice instead, twice, and the mule pushed past his knee and drank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:15:He was nineteen, and he had no Path. Everybody else whom he knew had been given one at fourteen, at a Kindling, and had been ranked by it ever since; his own Arbiter had woken on its brass plate that day, and taken eleven seconds over him, and gone dark. What answered him instead were fragments. There were ten of them now, pieces of other people's Paths, each one taken in the middle of a fight that somebody had meant, and each one written down in an old man's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-17-The prices were what he knew best, since he had carried all ten down the roads of this country for a hundred and thirty days and used them as seldom as he could. He could have said to a grain what each fragment charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-45-He said nothing more about it. Cael had been priced by experts for as long as he could remember, and he waited for the figure; the man only turned the block over and began on its other cheek.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:47:Over the man's head the ferry's cable hung bare from bank to bank, with rime on it. She had made her last crossing. No post would come over until the thaw, so that a letter to Denvash and another to a yard in a western city would lie unanswered for as long as the ice chose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-49-Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-59-They were in the store behind the chimney at noon, the five of them, which was two more than it had been built to hold. Karis had the table. Lira sat on a meal-sack with the great boot stuck out before her, and Brom filled the doorway, since there was nowhere else that he would go. Seln had the sill of the one small window, and had contrived to take up no room on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md-63-The kit's case lay open on the deal. It had come up out of a hole in the ground sixteen days ago, on Brom's good shoulder, and nobody had yet done more than look into it. There was the cord, coiled, with a knot at every fifth stride. There was the mallet. And there was the stake, which Karis had lifted out and was holding in both hands, as a person holds something that belongs to somebody else.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-7-He stood at the basin now, with his back to the rock, and held the thing low against his body, where the wind could not get at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-11-He had known that day. He had written it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-15-So he had stopped. He remembered it without any pride: his hands, and his breath, and the eyes that he had not been quick enough to take off the pencil. The boy had seen. He saw most things, though he was slow to say so. Seln had said that he had read it, which was true, and had gone to a window and looked at nothing for the rest of a lamp.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-17-He had not been certain. That was the reason that he had given himself, and it had been a good one for two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:A date read once, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, in another person's pencil, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages to find it otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. So might she, for all that she never did. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where it could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-21-He read it again now, properly.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-37-A fact could be filed. One kept it, and said nothing, and let it lie against the day when it should be useful; and he had kept a great many so. It was what a desk did. He had cut a hundred and forty-seven notches in two hazel wands to count what a desk would do with a clerk's packet, and had been wrong about the manner of it; but he had not been wrong about desks. There was an office somewhere that had held six years of sheets where nobody would receive them. He had been reading its work all morning. He did not care to find, at his age, that he had become a small copy of it, with a locked case for a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-41-A fact could be reported. That was the second way, and it was the one that an honest man would be expected to take. He had only to walk back to the rock and say eleven words.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-83-It lay the wrong way up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:Cael saw that before he saw anything else, and for a breath it steadied him. A clerk who set a paper before a person turned it to face him. This one had been turned the other way, with its printed head toward the far side of the rock, as papers had lain on a registrar's counter at Denvash, and on a magister's desk, and on a table on a bluff. It was how he had learned to read. Somebody had noticed that, and had laid the sheet so on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-87-He was not one short. That came next, like the blood coming back into a hand. There had been nothing the matter with his counting. A sheet had been out of the row, in a quiet man's sleeve, and here it was.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-105-They were one date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md-109-He had been waiting for it, he found, without knowing that he waited. A quiet man had stopped like a clock over that date, by a lamp. There was only one day in the registry's whole count that could have stopped him so on Cael's account.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-263-"It's a rock," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:265:"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-267-"That knee wants the forenoon as well," said Oryn. "I'd have stopped you at the next water, whether there'd been a table or not."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-11-Cael had washed his hands with the rest, and he stood at the slab's far side, where every sheet lay the wrong way up to him, as paper always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:He had not known where else to stand. He had touched neither the cord nor either strip of brass for a hundred and twelve days, by his own ruling, since a thing that measured had read one way in four hands and another in his. Paper was not a cord. A sheet said what it said, whoever turned it. He told himself so, and kept his hands on the rock's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-15-Oryn would not come near the slab at all.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-25-She had gone back to the uppermost row. She went through it a third time, quickly, looking only at the heads; and this time she did not count upon her fingers, as she had by the lamp. She sorted. Where the four signs at a sheet's head were the same four as on the sheet before, it stayed where it lay. Where they were another four, she turned its corner up. She could not read a sign of them. She could tell one group from another as she could tell Brom's face from Lira's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"I said by a lamp that I should give you a number when I had checked it in daylight," she said. "I have checked it, and I am not going to give one, and I owe you the reason. What I counted that night were groups of signs. I let you think that I was counting places, and I thought so myself." She laid her pencil down. "I do not know that. Two clerks may sign one place in two ways. One place may be entered under one group for its floor and under another for its fence. Until somebody can read a sign, a group is a group, and I shall not put my name to how many holes in the ground it comes to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-29-"There's four on the hide," said Brom.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-109-"No," said Karis. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:111:She had said it twice in an inn yard, a hundred and twelve days ago, to him. She said it now to herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-113-"I was about to tell you what a thing is from how it went out in me. I was the ruler, at those lines; I had no other. I shall not finish that."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-185-Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-189-"So it's nothing to do with which got fenced," said Brom.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-89-"He holds it," said Brom slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:91:"He's Iron, or he's Stone, or he's some small thing that he was given at fourteen and has kept in his pocket for two days. He's had it out before he's thought. Carters declare, on the ice." The factor spat over the wall. "And then they come up out of the sink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-93-"How many?" said Seln.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-7-Karis came in before the smith's first hammer, with her gloves on already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:"I am packing," she said. "I said that I should count in daylight, on a table without elbows, and I have been awake since the middle of the night working out where such a table is to be had. It is not in this inn. So everything goes into the flat, in the order that he gave it, and the flat goes where I go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-11-She folded the oilcloth over the page along its own creases, and did not look at him while she did it. The broker's sheet went under the sixth row, and the straps drew down across a red stamp which said that the thing was to go by hand.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-101-They were on the landing an hour before noon, with the mist gone and the river a hand lower than it had been at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:103:It took that long to leave a place where five people had lain for a hundred and forty-eight nights. Brom carried the three boxes down from the hearth's end one at a time, roped as he had roped them in the night, and slung them on the brown mule with the beckets outward, while she stood for it at the shed's door with her ears back, like a person being measured for a coat. Karis walked beside the load with the hide under one arm and the flat under the other, and would give neither of them to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-105-Cael left the word with Pike, as he had promised a better captain that he would: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever lay beyond it, with no day put on the coming back. Pike wrote nothing. He said that he had a season line of theirs with no number against it, and had stopped expecting to fill it by the week.

