editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:259:"I shall not say a number tonight. I have turned one row of six, by a lamp, and I have counted signs that I cannot read by telling one group from another. I should not put my name to such a count." She lifted her hand from the deal. "I shall say how many when I have checked it in daylight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md-261-Nobody spoke. The hide stood rolled in the corner by the meal-sacks, with four rings on it. Cael had walked round three of those rings, and had counted the bars of a fence about the fourth, and he had supposed that a calfskin held all that there was to hold. Somebody at a desk had known better than that for six years, and had written the same word in a narrow column each time, and had sent the sheet where nobody would receive it.
