editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:207:He kept to the road, since there was nothing else to keep to. It was a country without a wall or a gate or a board of hours, and for the first two days he met nobody at all. He slept under a pine. He learned to put the bad leg down straight and to let the stick take what the knee would not, and he learned that he could do ten miles so, and not eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md-209-The first was a woman on a mule, at a ford where two rivers ran together. There was a cairn on his bank and another on the far one, and they did not stand opposite. He was looking at that when she rode into the water from the other side and came over on the slant, cairn to cairn, with the stream never above the animal's knee. She was brown and spare, and her hat had the brim cut away on the right. A hatchet hung at her saddle with its edge turned outward.
