editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md-65-Pressure had begun to gather under his breastbone at the first sight of the raft. It was the narrow hard push that he had from the Bronze from Ardenmere, and at sixty yards it would have gone through a foot of ice as an awl goes through leather. He had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It went on, into whatever stood behind; and behind that ice were a deck, and a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:*There?* asked Anchor, of the post. It was a fair question. A point laid on that oak would have held it as a nail holds a board, for as long as he could hold the point. He had held one for sixty counts once, and a second time to thirty with his other hand shut on a drum to keep himself upright. While he held it he would have nothing else. And he would be kneeling at a post with his palm on it, working the registry's own craft, before forty people who meant to cross that river to the west as soon as it would carry them.
