editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md-69-He told both of them no. Wind wanted his feet, and he did not trouble to answer it.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-5-She had sat all the cold term on a baulk of squared oak at the slip's head, with blocks under her bilges, and the blocks had been knocked out in the night. Now she rode on six rollers of peeled alder. The ferryman's boy ran round from her stern with each roller as it came free and laid it down again under her bow, and Brom walked backward down the slope with his good shoulder against her and his boots sliding in the tallow. Cael had a line from her rail, with two turns of it round a pile, and he gave it out a hand at a time when he was told to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:7:He had his back to the far bank, and he was aware of it the whole time, as a man is aware of a door behind him. Three nights ago a small pale woman had asked him, at a board, to go back over that river, to a town inside the Line with a gate and a book in it. He had said that he would rule, and he had not ruled. Nothing could come over that water to change what a ruling would cost, and nobody could go over it to pay, until this boat swam.
