editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:71:That left the man by the wall, and Cael did not turn his head. A clerk had sat at a copying table for twelve years, under the eyes of officers like this one, with forty orders hidden where no inspection would look; and in all that time there was one thing that he had kept more carefully than the orders. It stood on a roll somewhere. He had never said it to a person who could have sent for the roll.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:91:"The four who were enrolled at a house above Ostrand. Not you. And not me; I was never enrolled there. I was employed. What waits for me is older, and it is not kept in a drawer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:261:Seln was looking at the hill. He had told one person, and he had chosen which. Cael understood why it had not been the one with the book; a thing that went into that book against a man's name would stay there, and Seln had spent fifteen years watching what became of names in books. Teague had dug forty-one holes for somebody else's posts before the frost. Two of his own stood a hand out of true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:"He begins with this. Four signs in a row, and after them the number, thirty-two. It is how he names the place that he is standing in." Her finger did not move. "They are a clerk's signs. They are not a carver's; I have forty-one kinds of mark from three floors, and these are not cut like any of them. Seln read registry paper for fifteen years, and he does not know the index that they are taken from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:197:A man who had been posted from town to town for fifteen years had been written into a book in every one of them, always by some other hand, and under a grade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:"Bronze," said Seln mildly. "Nobody has asked me in fifteen years. They had it on a roll."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"I have gone fifteen years without leaving one of those on anything," said Seln. He was looking at the strip with real interest. "It was the second thing I was taught. I find that the hand does not want to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:He had it ready, and it was not the one that she expected. On any floor he had ever stood on he would have decided this himself, and in a breath, since the plan was always his. But the thing in front of him was a weight and a gap, and whether a back could take the one over the other; and there was a person on that shelf who had known such sums since he was twelve years old, and it was not Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:15:He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:53:He had been keeping off it since noon. It had stood at the back of everything that he did that day, as a figure stands at the back of a room: a square bar, driven at the far end of a measure, with a stamp on its neck that he had seen on every door he had walked through for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:111:"I've a knife and fifteen years. It was never most of what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:247:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:He had spent fifteen years as the first of those, and he knew what it was to write a true line at another person's bidding without being told what the line was for. He had written sixty-three subjects into bundles in that way, each of them a person with a face that he could have drawn; and he had sent every bundle up a stair to people whom he had never once seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:He let himself go back to the corridor, which he allowed seldom. It was distempered green to the height of a man's shoulder, in a district seat three postings and twelve years behind him, and he had stopped dead in the middle of its floor with an armful of somebody's travel endorsements. A clerk had put his head out of a side door to ask whether he was unwell. He had not been unwell. He had understood, in the space between one step and the next, what a certain run of ledgers was that he had copied the year before as dull upkeep; and whose question it had been all along.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:283:He had been taught four grades of pressure, in their order. There was the record, and then the instrument, and then the knock, which was paper that nobody would own. Last came the hand, which was a road with no paper at all. He had believed for fifteen years that the hand was the worst of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:303:For twelve years the thing in the case had been like weather to him. It had fallen on forty children, and on a good many more before them, as rain falls on a field; and a man does not go looking for the face of rain. He files what it did; and Seln had filed it, and had been very good at that, and it had let him sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:325:He had lost an argument on a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second night past the Line. He had proposed that the watches should be his to arrange as he judged best, since he had fifteen years of judging them; and a girl with a ruled table had told him that the rota was not set aside for anybody's judgment, his own included. He had been angry for an hour and grateful ever since. A man who has decided things alone for fifteen years does not stop because he has resolved to; he stops when four people will not let him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:327:And there was the other reason, which he had given a boy in a loft. He had told him that he would sooner see a healer warned of what might be done to her than be used as the reason for keeping her ignorant. If that was good for a healer, it was good for a crew. He could not carry the danger off in the night and leave them to find a cold place under a tree. That was the service's way, and it had been his own, every week, for fifteen years: to know what was best for a subject, and arrange it, and say nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:273:"It is a good word for a report," said Seln. "I should have been glad of more words of that kind, in fifteen years." He considered the leaf. "There will be another name for this in the west, on the docket of a tin. I never read the docket. I do not know what they call it, and I shall not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:277:He wrote four short lines, in the small square hand of a man who had filled other people's files for fifteen years, and read them over as he read anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He had wanted a face, and had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods. This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:83:He had seen people stand so before him for fifteen years, on the other side of a counter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:89:He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:169:"Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another." Seln's mild voice had an edge in it that Cael had heard only once before, by a grey stone, the night before. "I signed for a satchel and four keys in my first posting, and I answered for every one of them for fifteen years. A surveyor answers for this. If he had walked out of that door on his own feet, he would have carried it, or he would have come back for it. And the people who sent us here knew that it was lying. They wrote *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper. They left it where it was for as long as they wanted a thing in this country that somebody could be sent to fetch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:"You are setting your cord beside his, and his figure is in a registry book. I have spent fifteen years learning what that office's paper is worth. I should not care to see you rest a finding on a number of theirs."

