editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He took the registration card out of his coat and looked at it. Name, number, district of issue. On the classification line, where Pellin's stamp had come down, there was one word in square brackets, and nothing else: no tier, no rank, no Path. He thought about holding it out to the clerk at the booth, and he thought about the clerk's face, and he put the card away again without getting any closer to the gate than the top of the hump.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-14.md:315:Standing there, he did not mind, though he had thought he would. He was in a book, in somebody else's careful hand, as exactly what he was and what he had done, and every word of it was something Vell had seen herself from this stool. It was a ledger he could check, and it had no word in square brackets in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-20.md:29:She laughed a little, as you laugh when somebody asks how you breathe. "You reach. It isn't a thing you do with your arms, so it's hard to say. It's more like going after a name you know you know, only on purpose, and it comes." She bent and pressed her palms flat to the dirt and talked to her knees. "Then it's there, at the edge, and it shows you. Tier and rank. Path. Your declarations, one under the other, in the order you got them. Sometimes there's a line at the foot of it if something's moving, if you're building toward the next rank and you're nearly there. All of it takes a breath. I hardly notice I've done it." She came up and set her hands on her hips and leaned back until something in her spine clicked. "You'll have yours soon enough, once whatever's stuck comes unstuck. You'll be bored of it in a week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:The book knew what he had done. Every line of it was something Vell had seen from her stool, in her own hand, and every word was true. It did not have a word in square brackets in it anywhere. It had *unrated*, and *vouched*, and *over Dessa*, and a line underneath that he was not supposed to have read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:The first sheet was a copy of the registration. *Caelen Hesk-ward. Registration 41-7843-V.* The certifying office was on a street called Weaver's Row, in Denvash, and the officer was a Warden-Adjunct named Pellin, whose signature was small and quick and gave nothing away. The date of the Kindling was in the autumn, fifty-odd days ago. On the line for the classification there was one word in square brackets, stamped, and no tier, no rank and no Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:The others had settled round him while he read. He felt them do it without looking. Karis was on his right, holding her shut notebook against her chest in both hands. Brom had taken the place behind him and to the right where the crowd would have pushed in, so that it could not. And Lira was at his left shoulder, near enough to touch. She had been there at the slate in the circuit house at Ardenmere when he was a boy with nothing written after his name, and she was here, and he could not remember a single board in between when she had stood anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:Cael saw that before he saw anything else, and for a breath it steadied him. A clerk who set a paper before a person turned it to face him. This one had been turned the other way, with its printed head toward the far side of the rock, as papers had lain on a registrar's counter at Denvash, and on a magister's desk, and on a table on a bluff. It was how he had learned to read. Somebody had noticed that, and had laid the sheet so on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:141:*A thing kept is a wall.* An old woman with a stick had taught him that, over a chandler's, with a cat on her knee. *A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.* He had said those lines on nineteen first mornings. He had kept the case shut for twelve years and it had been a very good wall: it had stood between him and every person he was ever sent to watch, and he had sat behind it and written true things, and been safe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:65:Nobody hurried him. They held their hands up and waited. It was his case and his twelve years and his name on a roll somewhere, and if his hand did not go up, then theirs would come down, and none of them would ever mention it again. Cael did not know which way it would go. He could have borne either, and that was new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:103:"What you have just told me, all of it, in order. Twice more, if there is ever a need, and you will know if there is, but not a third time." He said it as he would have said a rule of the wing to a first-year who had asked about floor hours. "Every telling is a copy, and a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known. I have kept the case twelve years by never once describing it to anybody, and you will keep this by telling it as seldom as you can bear to." He paused. "I am not ungrateful. I am only telling you the price, because nobody told me mine until I had paid it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:254:He had written everything for years. Every notice word for word, every price. The book was where a thing went to be true. And Seln had sat across the table and asked where it was written, and when he had answered, Seln had said *Under the bed*. In a voice that cost nothing, he had said, *A thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known.* The case had been kept twelve years by a man who had never once described it to anybody. Cael did not think he would last twelve hours if he started writing it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:"Then I'm found in a week," said Seln. "That is not a complaint, only the arithmetic. There is one leaf in that case that is not a copy. Somebody will ask who could have stood in a district's top-floor records room for five weeks, twelve years ago, and the service keeps its posting rolls very well. They would have me by the Seventh-day, and they would ask me what else I kept." He looked at the satchel between his feet. "After that it is no longer forty children and a leaf two hundred years old. It is an officer of the monitoring service who stole from a press, and the broadsides would print that instead. It is a better story, and it is also true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:"He begins with this. Four signs in a row, and after them the number, thirty-two. It is how he names the place that he is standing in." Her finger did not move. "They are a clerk's signs. They are not a carver's; I have forty-one kinds of mark from three floors, and these are not cut like any of them. Seln read registry paper for fifteen years, and he does not know the index that they are taken from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:71:That left the man by the wall, and Cael did not turn his head. A clerk had sat at a copying table for twelve years, under the eyes of officers like this one, with forty orders hidden where no inspection would look; and in all that time there was one thing that he had kept more carefully than the orders. It stood on a roll somewhere. He had never said it to a person who could have sent for the roll.

