editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:"No." It came through shut teeth, very distinct. "It does not go anywhere. I told that girl so. It stands where I stand, and I may not lift a foot; if I shift so much as a heel, the whole of it is gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:211:He did not fall. Cael had thought that he would, and was half way down the bank to him. But the man had both hands on the stick. He bent over it until his forehead was almost on his knuckles, and stayed so, with his feet where they had been. They had not moved since the stick came down. He lifted the left boot now, an inch, and set it down, as if to learn whether it was still his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:They did not all sit. Vastin stood in the gap of the fold, where the hurdle went, with his back against the stone and the bound leg out before him. He had come six miles from the spring on that stick, at the tail of the column, and had been offered a mule twice; and he had the grey look of a man who is counting what is left in him and finding it sufficient by a very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:"Arm," she said to Lira. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:"Fifteen days. It held a hound yesterday, by what I'm told, and it's none the worse. It's slow, and it'll be slow for as long again. They've stone floors where you're going, and stairs. Don't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:159:"Knee. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:165:"This is the first day. There are twenty-nine after it. It's stood a spring and six miles of road, and it's no worse than I left it, which is more than you deserved." She sat back. "Stick; binding; no kneeling. No walls. You'll want to carry something tomorrow, when those boxes come off my mule. You're not to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:193:Cael had a thing in him, since noon, that had walked out of a row of paper and that he had not written down. He heard those three words fall on it; and he said nothing, and was aware of a small grey man at the hearth who said nothing either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:205:"You were told not to fall on one," said Oryn. She was drawing the mule's girth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:207:"Then I'll fall on the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:He had kept his hand a hand's width from that page for the whole of a lamp, so that he might still say no to it. He had ruled on a slip instead, at a plank, in daylight. He had walked three days up a road behind a job. Somewhere on that road the no had gone, without his having marked the stride; it had gone at a spring, perhaps, between two hands laid flat on a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"I said by a lamp that I should give you a number when I had checked it in daylight," she said. "I have checked it, and I am not going to give one, and I owe you the reason. What I counted that night were groups of signs. I let you think that I was counting places, and I thought so myself." She laid her pencil down. "I do not know that. Two clerks may sign one place in two ways. One place may be entered under one group for its floor and under another for its fence. Until somebody can read a sign, a group is a group, and I shall not put my name to how many holes in the ground it comes to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:171:"The class of persons to whom it may not be shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:223:He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Three days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:35:"Then stand," said Oryn, "and you'll stand still, and you'll put your weight on the big one's shoulder and not on that stick. I'm not kneeling on a frozen road to have you fall on me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:71:"It was. Off. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:255:There was an outcrop of the dark plated stone a furlong to the south, as big as a barn, and under its eastern face the ground was green, where a spring came out. It rose in a basin that somebody had built round with stones, a long while ago, and ran over the basin's lip and away down the moor in a thread of rushes. Beside it a single slab of the rock had fallen flat from the face. It lay level, waist-high on its own rubble, twelve feet long and half as broad, with the outcrop standing between it and the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:229:"May I ask a thing that I ought perhaps to have asked this morning?" Vastin had his bread in his hand, and he did not lower himself onto a stone until he had the question out. "You have told me what I am to do if one of them comes at me. You have not told me what I am to do if it comes at one of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:51:He looked at that squarely, standing over a spring on a moor with a sheet in his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
