editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the registry's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:65:The fifth column she had read by a lamp already. There were three clerks' hands in six years: a round one, and a cramped one that leaned backward, and one that made its capitals like gates. Each of them had written the same single word against every station of every place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write. He was a man who lived among instruments, whoever he was, and he knew what a device does. It does the same thing at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule." She had laid her pencil across the sheet's foot, to keep the wind from its corner. "That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:131:"The fifth is yours. You found the remark; I looked for what came after it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:"On the form that those four words were written upon, there is a space at the foot for remarks. On the next year's printing there is none. The rule that closed it off has been taken out of the block, and the fifth column runs to the foot of the sheet." His mild voice did not alter. "Somebody read them. It is the only sign in six rows that anybody read anything at all; and what he did, having read them, was to see that no surveyor should have room to write four more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:185:They had been last into it, and so uppermost. They got clear of the plates on the uphill side with their coats full of grit, and they stood for a breath on the frozen ruts with their heads low. Not one of them looked at the wall. Karis had it in her fifth line. Five of their kind had been killed in that place in less time than it takes to say so, and by nothing that made a sound. The only sound in the cut was a thing that could not be bitten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:83:"Five," said the factor, at Brom's stone. "I said five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:25:Five of them read it together from the left-hand post, since Lira and Brom had come round from the well, and Seln had been standing there already, with no wand under his arm. It was a strip of bark, like every other slip on that oak, in the square black hand. Beside the words Pike had drawn a little road going between two heaps, and on the road something with four legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:79:"Five," said Pike. He made the strokes at the slip's foot, and then he did not dip his pen again. He turned his shoulder to them. "And who's your healer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:"Five," said Seln. "Under the thorn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:125:They were grey on grey, and they did not move, and it took Cael the better part of a quarter-hour to be certain of the fifth. They lay as dogs lie that have nothing to wait for, each in its own scrape, with its nose toward the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:127:"Five is what can be seen from here," said Seln. "There is a strip under the north bank that this lip hides, perhaps four yards of it, the whole length of the cut. I cannot tell you what lies in it. I should not care to say five to a factor who has just told me five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:171:"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:233:*Yes*, he told the fifth of the ten, for the first time in earnest since a stair.

