editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:707:- **Deployment since the Line: TWENTY-THREE.** Fourteen is Tide on Oryn on the 113th (one sitting, short count then a minute of the long, counted as one). Fifteen to twenty-three are on the 114th: Anchor, looking, at the two stakes (dawn); then at dusk Shadow (once); the read, in the ground; Anchor, looking, for the knot (absent at fourteen feet, faint at twelve, sure at ten); Anchor, the unbinding, palm on the knot (no reach, rate or price assigned); Wind, one burst; Storm, one lane up the old rut; Compression at contact; Ember, one point on the knife. Nothing was used inside the line in sight of the rim; Tide was used on nobody inside any line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:710:- **The rim:** six, faceless — no seals, names or office; four crossbows and two without, the second Anchor hand one of the six (not the Ostrand-road woman: the same craft, a different weight in the laying). They waited forty paces outside the edge. Their bows are left disabled; their bolts are too stout for Oryn's bow. The crew's five bolts stayed five. The Stair slip's three hundred was taken and never paid. The second paper was lodged sealed with the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:711:- **Brom took a bolt** on the 114th (high; missed the lung and the big vessel; "the bolt's the plug"); Oryn drew it and closed it when her hands worked again, outside the line. Exact states are in the author's section below, as corrected after repair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:712:- **The Stair's chamber:** walls cut floor to roof in five courses, each as tall as a forearm; a line that is not on the Ring stands BETWEEN the courses, on its own, recurring "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride" — the Fallow's radius. (There is no continuous groove.) Karis paced the perimeter on the morning of the 115th and got two hundred. The open kit with its contents in taking-out order was found and is carried by Brom; **the case is untouched; the index and the stroke-and-bar are not decoded; nobody theorizes.** "Compact" is not said in this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:713:- **Oryn** was at Millrace on the 113th (her circuit brought her round; EXC-B7-003), crossed for a wounded patient on the 114th and went down under a held rim on the 115th; she is a stop on her route — never "sixth", never "join". **Teague** forfeited a slip to come, held the rim unasked on the 115th ("We'll hold it till you're up." / "Noted"); his crew is unnamed; the healer of no Path has not appeared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:715:- **Length:** the reads split; the coordinator ruled that the Stair's action is not cut for the map's 40,000 line, and repetition is removed instead. **Names:** none minted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:721:- **The ground at the Stair (flag; one picture for chapters 48–52).** A ridge runs north and south; the Stair road comes south along its top and drops off its western face. Halfway down, the slope eases into a brow, and there the carters' live ruts turn north and go the whole way round a shallow bowl some five hundred yards across. The edge of the Quieting lies forty paces inside the ruts. The old straight road, two grown-over ruts, runs on down from the brow, crosses the edge between two iron bars eight feet apart (the old one on the north side, the newer on the south, with Karis's stone between), and goes to the floor: a hundred and ninety yards of grass, then thirty of floor to the hole. The floor is a bare round plate sixty of Cael's strides across, with no upright thing on it. The hole is two strides square, at the middle; the first step is on its eastern side and the stair goes down westward, forty steps, cut and not laid, every edge unworn; the air changes at the twentieth. The chamber opens westward from the stair's foot: ten strides each way; its roof is the underside of the floor, a foot above Brom's head; dust "as thin as the bloom on a plum". The rim (the ruts) is two hundred and sixty yards from the hole and sixteen hundred paces and a little more round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:722:- **The chamber (flag; changed in repair r1):** ten strides square, as paced on the 115th; the roof is the underside of the floor above, without beam or crack, and stands **a hand above Brom's head** (Cael had called it a foot on the 114th, at a crouch; Brom's palm lies on it with the elbow bent). Dust as thin as the bloom on a plum. **The walls** are cut from floor to roof in five courses, each as tall as a forearm, in the same hand as the bands on the Fallow's stones (Karis can see the bars). **There is no groove.** What Karis keeps for the last of the oil is the protected finding: a line that is not on the Ring, on every wall, not in the courses but *between* them, on its own, recurring "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride… at the perimeter's radius." How that spacing is read off walls ten strides long is not glossed on the page (repair flag 2). She samples the walls on twenty-nine sheets, and twice walks the foot of a wall with the short cord, counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:723:- **The stair's air (changed in repair r1):** from the twentieth step down the air does not move; a lamp flame stands "like a nail" (Brom sees it in Karis's lamp before anybody names the step; Seln: "The air stops there."). In the chamber it is colder than the frost above and "smells of nothing" (Oryn). The gate bow's eighth ranging bolt lies on the thirty-first step until Lira takes it up on the 115th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:724:- **Hound sign at the Stair (flag):** old runs in the grass on the north side, unused since the frosts began; no den that Seln can find from outside. He says that he does not know why and will not guess. No stillhound appears in this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:728:- **The rim's bolts (flag): thirteen.** Gate bow eleven (Brom's shoulder; one during the run; eight ranging shots at the hole, the eighth down the stair to the thirty-first step; one at Cael outside the stone at dusk, which misses); north bow one and south bow one, lofted during the run; west bow none. They are too stout for the channel of the healer's bow (Brom tries one along the stock). On the 115th the boy lays the recovered shafts in a row by the four bows, Lira adds the one from the stair, and the head cut from Brom's bolt lies by the stone; the figure thirteen is given once, in the night log of the 114th. Teague reads eleven stirrup-prints "and a twelfth half made" on the brow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:729:- **How the six leave (flag):** each bow has its string cut and the nut drawn from its lock, and is laid down on its side; the maker's stamp has been filed off the stock and the place oiled; no armoury number. Seln: "bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." Nothing else is left at any station. The four bows lie in a row by the stone on the 115th, one in two pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:742:- **Lira's bowls (flag):** "We lay six bowls… Brom puts six out every night and takes one back." / "I'll eat out of it. I'll not sit in a place." At the Stair she eats out of the last of the six, standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:746:- **Injuries, as fixed on the page.** Brom: a bolt through the back of the right shoulder, a hand below its top, the head out under the collar-bone by a thumb's length; taped round the shaft in the chamber with what was left of Lira's ankle tape; outside the stone Seln cuts the shaft below the head and Oryn draws it; mended in an hour; the arm bound across his chest on the 115th; nothing on that side "till I've said so". Lira: her ankle goes at about the twentieth step and the slung arm takes the wall; the lowest mark of each row opens; bound with a strip of Cael's shirt; closed in a quarter of an hour at the stone; the sling tied shorter, the hand at the collar-bone. Cael: both hands and forearms cold and dead for the evening (Shadow); the right forearm struck (it bends by morning); a weal across the left palm, wound in tape, not mended. Oryn: three mendings on the 113th; two on the night of the 114th ("There isn't a third in me tonight").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:747:- **The 115th.** Teague's four on the rim: Teague on the brow, the spear south, the boy west, the bow north; Teague lends his horn lantern. The pacing runs from the stone northward with the Quieting on the right hand; Lira carries the cord's far end and finds the edge with her foot; Seln cuts the turfs; the old stakes stand within a boot of them; the round is the Fallow's, cord for cord, with the same odd strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:756:**Calendar.** Late afternoon of the **hundred and fifteenth day past the Line**, at the stone on the eastern edge of the Long Stair, in full sun. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term", "frost", "the thaw", "the snow", "a dry month"). Days on the page: 111, 112, 113, 114, 115; in Seln's window, the night of the 112th; in Teague's, the 113th, 114th and the dawn of the 115th. Weather: hard frost every night, ringing roads, ice along Lowmarch's bank; no snow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:758:**Where everyone is.** The six at the stone, with two mules (the brown, the crew's; the mill's grey jenny, Oryn's for now), three boxes, and the kit on the turf. Teague and his three have gone back up the ridge toward Millrace. The six from the rim went off north, south and west at dusk on the 114th and have not been seen since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:760:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-three**, by his own log (fourteen on the 113th; nine on the 114th; none on the 115th). **Shadow used twice in all** (the Ring; the Stair), unseen by anybody outside the circle who could carry it; it is shut again. Anchor: looked twice and unbound once. The tenth has now read one other person, with her leave, at one sitting; **he has still read himself once only**, and owes Oryn two more readings on different days and then all of it; she told him "not tonight" on the 113th. What he found in himself is unsaid and unnamed aloud. Body: the right forearm struck (bends on the 115th); a weal across the left palm, taped, unmended; hands warm again. He has said *Noted* twice to Teague. He holds the kit (Brom carries it). Hesk's satchel: the log, the old volume, the notebook, the letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark. Three letters still unanswered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:762:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm: two marks reopened on the stair wall on the 114th, closed again that night; slung shorter, the hand at the collar-bone; under the order until first snow, and "not going to hold anything else this season".** Right ankle taped, unmended, gone once more on the stair; her tape is used up. She ran two hundred and twenty yards on the released shin. She carried the healer's bow down and held the stair with it for a day without loosing. She found sixty-two stations of an edge with her foot. Her "walk away" is entered, and she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:764:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **Right shoulder: bolted through from behind on the 114th, mended once that night; the arm bound across his chest; nothing on that side until Oryn says.** He carried the kit up forty steps on the left. He has the healer's bow again, its hook, and **five bolts**. He has quoted Rooke. He lays six bowls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:766:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point set, on her lamp, outside the stone on the night of the 114th. Holds: **forty sheets** (eleven of the Ring, twenty-nine of the Stair); **forty-one kinds, the same at both**, entered and underlined; **the line between the courses** (not on the Ring; on every wall of the chamber; recurring at ten cords and a piece, two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, the perimeter's radius), shown on the eastern wall's sheets; the Stair's perimeter table (the Fallow's round, to the stride); the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, written down from Seln's reading; the leaf of the trials, inside her coat; the calfskin, with *guess* struck out beside the Stair's ring and still standing beside the far south-eastern one; the Ternhall brass, out of its cloth and held over the hide. Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid. She has said that the floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size, and nobody took it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:772:**Teague.** He stood for the five at Lowmarch and sat at "eleventh". He hung the second paper on the nail. He took and forfeited a four-mark slip to be near; he saw one boy, outside the edge, declare a Wind, a fire and a third thing that he cannot name; he said only that he saw a man walk out of the Quiet declaring, and asked nothing. His three walked the ruts by lantern and brought in three crossbows. He held the rim through the 115th and lent a lantern. He still has a day's posts on the forty-mark fence. His crew is unnamed; the healer of no Path has not appeared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:774:**The six.** Unseen since dusk on the 114th. No face, name, seal, office or word. They know that a fence failed at its knot on the edge, and that a boy came out of the old road and declared, outside, more than one thing. They saw nothing work inside. They did not touch the boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:776:**Objects and money.** Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand**, unchanged. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against the broker's mark, and will not be marked. The Fallow hundred lies there too. **The kit:** case, pacing cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, mallet; the ledger apart, with Seln. Four crossbows by the stone, strings cut and locks empty, one broken; the rim's recovered shafts in a row beside them (thirteen loosed, by the log of the 114th), useless to the healer's bow. Karis's lamp filled; Teague's lantern returned. Owed still: the ferryman's rope; three replies; Hesk's parcel has not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:778:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Eight lines**; the eighth said twice in the crew's hearing on the 112th. **Eleventh; Teague ten up.** The Stair paper carries five strokes and **HOLD, on Pike's own mark**, said to the room and to be said to the meadow. Pike has said that his book has no column for what the eighth line means.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:780:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. That the fragments answered at the Stair: the six. Which fragments, by Path: the six; where the ninth came from: the five. Teague and his three: a declaration outside the edge, of more than one kind. Pike and the room: that six went in at the Fallow and six came out. The registry's side: as above. Nobody has said why anything answers him inside. Nobody has put a verb between a made floor, an age with no ruler, the same round twice, a circle whose size the carvers knew, and a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:790:- **Research custody:** Seln has the field ledger inside his coat; Karis has eleven Ring sheets plus twenty-nine Stair sheets, forty-one kinds, the trials leaf, the perimeter table, the calfskin with the Stair's *guess* struck and the south-eastern *guess* still standing, and the Ternhall straightedge (52:165–209,247–305,355–369).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:796:**Movement 7 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the ten audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed; ch52's three tracked passages restaged (scene 2 re-entered on the bolt at the thirty-first step; the kit read from the cord inward); the design confirmed once; twice-told states removed from ch49–51 with the night log whole; the closing log's "five years ago" made to stand (the ledger's eight stations are the old irons, five cold terms by Seln; the stroke-and-bar is "two visits", the later undated; nothing decoded); a register pass. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES** — seven applied by the coordinator: **what Cael asked at Millrace's bench is corrected to the source's actual distinction** (leave to touch a boy's wrist while Oryn held it — refused; on the road he asks to read HER — granted, "a different question"), with the bench's count and the day's log to match; and ch52 says Karis reads the radius figure from scaled divisions within the lone line, not as a spacing along the wall. All three tracked passages RESOLVED, no new tracking. Gates 0; overlap as below. 42413 words (the length ruled by the coordinator: the Stair's action not cut for the map's 40,000 line). Author-sourced sections above refreshed from the post-repair report; where they still describe the bench request as a request to read Oryn, the Tide ruling above governs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:804:- **Deployment since the Line: TWENTY-SEVEN at book end.** Twenty-three after the Stair; then at the Hall on the 119th the read under water (24), Compression taken standing (25), an Anchor point laid by hand in the breach and held (26); and on the 120th Tide on Oryn, asked and granted, one sitting (27). The Anchor point set as a trial on the 118th is NOT counted ("I'll not call a trial a use"), which agrees with the protected inventory: Anchor used twice this year, once each way (the Stair's unbinding; the Hall's holding). Self-readings are not counted. **Shadow was not used**; it has been used exactly twice in the book and remains sealed; Oryn does not know Seln is its source.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:808:- **The sites and the line:** the Ring (the Fallow), the Stair, the Hall, and the fourth, taken as lying on one line "as near as this skin can show"; no compass bearing is claimed for the whole. Only two maker sentences exist outside protected text: "Someone made this pattern." (said once; nobody answers) and "a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one." Karis's hypothesis is still unstated; her window ends on the pencil stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:809:- **Sheets: fifty-two** (eleven Ring, twenty-nine Stair, twelve Hall). The survey kit is on the table at Lowmarch: the case in tarred canvas, a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, never driven, and a mallet; the dead man's ledger, shut, is Karis's (Seln gave it to her). The case of Seln's is shut and untouched; nothing is decoded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:819:- **The brass on the hide (flag).** The Ternhall strip is slid down along itself with a pencil dot (a ruler "as long as its owner is patient"). Laid through the Fallow's ring and the Stair's, it runs into the carter's water-strokes and passes his third blank **a finger outside** Karis's pencilled ring. She moves nothing on the 115th. On the 120th she rubs the ring out and draws it where the cord put it, with *moved; paced.* On the 123rd she adds a fourth ring "at the very edge of the skin", with no word beside it; by the 131st a fine pencil line is ruled through the three centres and the fourth ring and off the hide. **What a ruler on a map of days is worth:** "a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm… a strip of country a mile wide." On the ridge she carries the line by ranging: Seln a furlong ahead, the last resting-place behind, moved until the three lie in a row. No compass direction is claimed for the line as a whole (ruled KEEP).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:821:- **The road to the Hall (flag):** the Stair road beyond the low ground, south and east under the ridge, for a day; then a grown-over track with a tree in one rut, for a day; then no track for some hours; then the track's last furlong straight down to the reeds. A mild spell on the 116th–118th; hard frost again from the night of the 120th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:835:- **Teague, days 115–121 (flag):** **he did not set the weir's third stake; the ford-keeper did not mark the shingle; the four marks were lost, as Movement 7 closed it.** He sent word to Pike by the mill's carter ("five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay"); followed the trail from the Stair for three days "for what's behind them, if there's anything", walking back a mile along it at each dusk; found nobody on it. He hears Karis's sentence and Cael's ruling on the 121st, watches the brass run off the hide, and asks nothing. His five reach Lowmarch before the five do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:836:- **Seln's book-keeping:** the ledger was in his coat from the 115th to the 127th. In his window: no name in it; "a field ledger is signed for at a counter, and the name stays at the counter"; his own issue sheet read *Returned or accounted for*. He gives it to Karis on the fourth day of the road home with "It is a field book. It ought to lie with the papers of the field." She will enter where it was found, when, and by whose hand it came to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:839:- **Pike's first snow (flag; ruled KEEP):** "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw." He has done forty that day. The ninth line is given in narration (a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting; taken; six gone in; instruments found, brought up and kept by the crew that found them; six come out). The clerk "has been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to", and is given nothing. Pike turns the ledger toward Cael for the first time in this edition.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:851:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven** (the ford 1; the Ring 12; Oryn on the ridge 1; the Stair 9; the Hall 3; Oryn at the marsh 1). The trial point at the Hall's kerb on the 118th is logged and not counted. Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor: looked three times; unbound once (the Stair); a point set twice, of which one is the uncounted trial and one held the breach. Tide: Oryn twice, each time by her leave; himself three times (102, 119, 120); nobody else; never inside a line. **The still place:** his old log label, now a location under the breastbone, read three times; nothing moves in it; all ten go round it and none through; they do not pile against it; not the Quiet; not a fragment; told to Oryn at once and in order on the 120th, and to the other four in three sentences on the 121st; he has been told to write it down and stop touching it, and has not touched it since. Body: ribs quiet since the morning of the 129th; right forearm and left palm no longer remarked; calf a closed seam. Hesk's satchel: the log (the entries of the 118th, 119th, 120th, the fork, the crest, and the inventory of the 129th), the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and note, Ephram's and Vell's letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark, and Oryn's letter on bark. **He has Hesk's brass straightedge, and it reads true in his hand.** He has said "Noted" to Teague three times. He has not answered Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:855:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **Right shoulder: bolted on the 114th and mended; opened on the Hall's wall on the 119th and mended a second time ("It will not hold three times this season"); bound on the 120th by Teague's healer and Oryn in four yards of webbing, with a drover's turn; the knot not to be undone except by that woman, who looked at it on the 129th and said "It'll do."** He carries the kit on his left side. He lays six bowls and, since the letter, has left the sixth out. He took the fence: "Ours,".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:863:**Teague.** Unread and unasked. **Four marks poorer for the weir slip, which he forfeited.** He knows: six went into the Fallow and came out; one walked out of the Stair declaring more than one thing; six came out of the drowned hall wet; a strip of brass runs through three places on a hide and off its edge. He has asked "What are you." once and been answered "I don't have one." He knows nothing of the mechanism or of anything working inside a line. His term: "After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." He has given the five the forty-mark fence and has the cold road. His crew is unnamed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:865:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Nine lines** under the five names; the ninth is the Stair, with *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The season line said and written. **No number against it.** **Eleventh on the cloth, unchanged.** He has turned the ledger toward Cael once. The Fallow hundred and the Stair's three hundred lie in his box unmarked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:869:**The Quieting, as known.** Four sites on one edge of brass. The Fallow Ring (stake 31), the Long Stair (32, on two bars, one with the stroke and the bar; the same mark on the kit's unused stake) and the Drowned Hall (33, old, no second) entered. The fourth seen and paced from outside on the 123rd: twice the radius by the bend of its cordon; stakes numbered 34 with the stroke and the bar, a pace apart; old ruts kept a hundred and ninety paces off; a standing grey shape that nobody could make out. **Not entered, not named, not dated, and not tried: no Arbiter has been taken to that line and what it does is unknown.** At the other three the live Arbiters stop at the line and Cael's fragments answer inside; whether a line twice the size does anything more is Lira's question and is unanswered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:871:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. The still place: the six. That fragments answer inside a line: the six. The Compact's side: a completion report on the Fallow; what the rim saw at the Stair; and now a clerk's packet saying that a crew kept a kit. Nobody outside the six knows that anything works inside. Nobody has said why. Nobody has put a verb between a made circle, an age with no ruler, four on a line, and a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:889:- **Karis/research:** Ember, Iron Rank Three; voice whole; 52 sheets split 11/29/12; forty-one kinds at the Ring/Stair and thirty-three seen on the sampled Hall wall; four copies of the three-site leaf; carter's map with four unlabeled/aligned circles; Ternhall straightedge; kit field ledger; hypothesis unspoken and unentered (`manuscript/chapter-57.md:3-87`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:17-27,239-255`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:892:- **Board/work:** five names remain eleventh; nine completed lines; no number on the first-snow season line; Stair price says “three hundred; taken; not paid”; Teague's exact term is “After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes.”; the five take the forty-mark fence, whose day's posts and far side remain to do (`manuscript/chapter-59.md:167-247`). Teague's four-mark weir slip remains forfeited after fix 8.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:893:- **Money/accounts:** ninety-six living-purse marks (102 less six); sealed cart-and-gelding reserve still unopened in the third box; Fallow hundred and Stair three hundred remain in Pike's box and are not crew-payable debts; ferryman is owed sixty yards of four-strand rope, not wanted before thaw; fence not begun (`manuscript/chapter-52.md:357`; `manuscript/chapter-59.md:145-157,175-179,241-247`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:7-11,31`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:257:"Then this stone is the objective, as the other was. Whatever is seen, whatever is heard, the word is *Back*, and every one of us comes to it by the straightest way." Seln looked at each of them in turn. "It is a good plan for a place with nobody in it. I do not know of a good plan for the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:45:He owed a healer two readings of himself with the tenth fragment, on two different days, with a night between; and after those he owed her the whole of it at once. She had set it at a well that morning, with a pannier on the coping. Nine days had gone by since the first reading, in a loft, and he had let every one of them pass. She had seen why from a stride and a half. He had found a place in himself that did not move, and he had been afraid of it, and he had liked it better as a line on a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:115:It was a thin house. There were perhaps twenty people on benches that had held forty, and every one of them had heard that line on the hundred and first day, when it was written. They had not then had the five to look at. Cael felt the room turn its attention on them as he had felt a yard do it once, at an academy on a hill. But this was no registry's look. Nobody's eyes went to where a tier ought to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:247:He had said four things to Cael at a ford, and every one of them had been a price. This was not a price. Cael went over it for the catch, since he had been taught in a hard school to look for one, and there was none in it: there was a place, and a day, and four people who would walk up a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:87:It was the wrong wall, and every one of them could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:"I know what I'll get. I've had it six times." She was counting already, under the words. "I've had my hands on every one of them this morning, to see what that place had done. I'll not sit down to my bread with one left out, only because he can't be read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:There was no sound in it, and nothing in it moved. He brought the ten to it one after another, as he had in a loft, and every one of them leaned aside and went by and closed again beyond. Those were the three things that he had counted on the night of his birthday; he counted them again, and they had not changed by a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:She had said the only true thing that she had. In eleven years she had put her hands into more bodies than she could number, the hurt and the whole and the dying, and every one of them had been all current, fast or slow, standing behind a break or running free. She had never met a still thing in a living person. The dying went still all at once, and everywhere. This was neither of those.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:169:"Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another." Seln's mild voice had an edge in it that Cael had heard only once before, by a grey stone, the night before. "I signed for a satchel and four keys in my first posting, and I answered for every one of them for fifteen years. A surveyor answers for this. If he had walked out of that door on his own feet, he would have carried it, or he would have come back for it. And the people who sent us here knew that it was lying. They wrote *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper. They left it where it was for as long as they wanted a thing in this country that somebody could be sent to fetch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:195:"Then there are distances, measured in cords, as she measures; his cord is that one, knotted at the fives. He begins at the edge. *North station to floor.* *East station to floor.* There are eight of those, by the points of the compass and the half-points, and against every one of them he has put the same figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:197:"His stations are the old irons," said Karis, to the wall. "There was a bar at each of those eight points this morning, scaled as the old one at the stone is scaled. I had a turf within a boot of every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:"No. Not down here, by a lamp, on my knee. I shall do it once, in daylight, on a level thing, with every one of you looking." She drew a breath that shook. "Lira. I want a sheet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:301:Nobody answered her. Cael saw every one of them leave it where it had fallen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:177:"Thirty-four. Thirty-four. Every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:215:"There are forty bars to the left of me that I have counted, and a great many more that I have not," said Seln. "I should want to walk every one of them by daylight before I advised anybody to stand inside them. I do not think that we have the bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:383:When the sun came up over the ridge behind him, there were four on the rim, and every one had his face bare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:The woman in the cut had laid nineteen points round him while he fought her, each as fine as a fishing line, from fifteen feet, with the stillness of a person threading a needle. Whoever had laid these was forty paces off. And they were thick: every one was as thick as his wrist, and there were three where she would have used thirty. It was the same trade in a different hand, as a ship's cable and a stitch are the same trade: this hand threw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:99:"Six, and four bows," said Seln. "I walked past every one of those places twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:He held them. He stood on a frozen road, with a bolt in Brom's shoulder and a healer staring at her own hand, and refused nine things one after another in the only country on the continent where every one of them would have answered. He had done as much once before, on a white floor, with a girl's arm in a hound's mouth. It was no lighter. Seln had told him that it would not be.

