   311	### Review record
   312	All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial; cold read; source-distance (3 tracked passages, none dense; probe 2% / 15%); plan / canon / protected-text audit (REPAIR; 102 exact fixes). Coordinator rulings against the audit, after checking the source and the locked Book 7: the cut's staging, the Ember use and the count of thirty-two KEPT (BOOK_MAP §8: private staging the author owns; the audit's 39 re-staging fixes declined); Oryn's past KEPT; Cael's touch of the page KEPT; the slip left as the packet quotes it; "every bar of a fence" KEPT (Book 7 ch58). Accepted from the audit: the six markers' speakers (two filled with the plan's own clause only); the loads for Movement 4; no fifth place or number; no second calendar; the remark's hand unidentified. Eleven fixes applied by the coordinator; the rest recomposed by the author. **Repair r1 in progress with the same author.**
   313	
   314	### New canon minted (author's list)
   315	- **The slip and Pike's rule (flag).** The clearance slip came over in the bag on the 277th; Pike drew a road between two heaps beside it. Twenty, "at the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it". **Pike will not write a clearance taken until a healer is said** (his own rule since his second year, when one of a crew of four bled for two days on a hurdle). His sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.*, the first column he has had for Oryn.
   316	- **Oryn's round this thaw (flag).** She goes west with the six as the slip's healer; from the fork-stone north to "five gates up along the hill-foot", one with a child that she was to have heard a cold term ago; then "down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above": her own round walked the wrong way about. Three weeks puts her at Millrace.
   317	- **Teague (flag).** The forty sacks went aboard on backs on the morning of the 278th; he is still on the east landing when the six cross, bound for the block on the cold road. He had first call on the slip by the cloth and left it. He has carried salt to the shoulder hold and led mules through its cut at noon with their heads held. "A man that holds a thing so doesn't leave off because nobody's asked him. I'd know what I had at my back." The word left with Pike: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever is beyond it; no day.
   318	- **The ferry and the rope (flag).** On the 278th she crossed in the forenoon with people, led beasts and carried sacks; no wheels. A copper a head, the mule a head. **The broker's cordage on the western landing is three-strand, hawser-laid. Brom refused it** ("A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks"). The sixty yards of four-strand are still owed.
   319	- **The border road.** One of two roads out of the western landing (the other runs south and west three hundred miles, to the ford and the station that Vastin crossed). It climbs through alders to a moor; a halting-ground in a fold by a beck, eleven miles out, with a dozen dead rings; a long shoulder of hill; the cut; four miles of high moor to a spring; six more to the fork-stone; a long rise of a mile and more.
   320	- **The four hands (flag).** Flat, palm down: *stand*. A shut fist: *seen*. Two fingers: *come on*. A hand turned over empty: *nothing*. The doctrine in one line: "Nobody declares unless they're meant to be loud. One of us, once, in a place that we've chosen beforehand."
   321	- **Stillhounds, added to Book 7's account (flag).** One paced the column for half a mile in the hour before sunrise ("dusk turned round") and stopped at a beck. **Karis's sixth line (16:117):** *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* The den at the cut had learned that whatever declares on that ice slides to the foot, and ran past the place of the declaring. Eight laired round the sink, three of them under the north bank where the lip hides four yards.
   322	- **The shoulder hold and its factor (flag).** A foot-track leaves the cart road at a cairn below the spur; a plain bar in a wall as thick as a cart; a small clean yard, a long low house, a salting-shed with empty hooks; no salt up since the first frost. The factor: spare, grey, a leather apron, a slate on a cord, a sheep-crook, pale eyes; a thumb-mark like a crook; **no name and no pronoun on the page**. The cut was made by "my grandfather's people". Three carts, a carter and two mules lost since the frost. "They never came up for a cart that kept its feet."
   323	- **The cut (flag; BOOK_MAP §8 extended).** A hundred strides, a cart and a man wide, rising east to west by the height of two men. South bank: thirty feet of scree at the pitch of a steep roof, with the hold's sheep-walk on its lip. North bank: low enough to step onto; beyond it fifty-five yards of scree down to the sink, a round bowl in shadow where water goes into the hill, iced grey and rotten, with a black unfrozen place under a lip of rock and scooped lairs round its rim. A seep at the cut's head runs down the middle of the road, a stride wide and a hand deep, under ice that never thaws in that shade. Three runs worn over the north bank in the upper half. Three wrecked carts at the foot.
   324	- **Iron Wall as shown (flag).** Named by Vastin ("It is called Iron Wall. It is on a roll, with a rank beside it"); **no tier is stated**. He steps into the road and brings the stick's foot down once; a sound "like a mallet on a cask" comes out of the road; a line runs from his boots across the cut and cracks the ice white, a hair wide; "the air stood up from it": a face from bank to bank as tall as the scree, seen only by the frost that lies on it. It holds eight hounds at a run without ringing or shaking. Limits said and shown: it does not go anywhere; **he may not lift a foot** ("if I shift so much as a heel, the whole of it is gone"); each blow on it comes down through him into the stiff leg; it goes out of him steadily, "the loudest thing that had been done on that road"; it shuts the road and not the banks. It falls when he lets it go. Oryn's two fingers: "You're at the floor." **"A Path of the tribunal floor… taught for fields; and a field is ground where a thing has been declared, a hearing, or a square, or a gate with a crowd at it." "I have stood on eleven such."** Karis has read of it in "a manual of engagement fields, with a diagram".
   325	- **The fight's count (flag).** Brom's rock at the plane's south end (one); Lira, two-handed, on the hound at Vastin's knee (two); one broken on the plane in the second run (three); Seln's knife on one standing in the runnel (four); Brom's rock on the other (five). Three over the north bank and through the sink's ice. Nothing came up.
   326	- **Bodies.** Vastin: four holes and a tear below the right knee, shut by Oryn; the joint "wrung like a cloth", bound with linen and two laths; **the bad knee is the right** (Movement 2 gave no side). Brom: the right boot torn from lacing to welt; two toes bruised, bound together, ten days.
   327	- **Oryn's past (flag; the earlier books say nothing).** "I was made in a city, inside the Line. It doesn't matter which." A house for the sick, forty beds, six healers; her Iron fourth rank is from it and is provisional because she "never stayed to have it made anything else". **A sheet on a board at each bed's foot: name, complaint, what had been spent, and a column for the patient's tier.** In a week of fever the spending went down the column from the top; a woman from the river sheds with no tier went to the bottom of the heap; Oryn went to her at night, off the sheet, and it was enough (**nobody died**); the woman who kept the house said that a healer who spends off the sheet has stolen from whoever stood next on it, "and I couldn't fault a line of them". She walked out of a gate in the coat she stood in and has not been through one since. Of stations: "There's one on the crown of every city inside the Line, with steps going up to it and a square in front."
   328	- **The spring (flag).** A furlong south of the road, four miles past the cut: an outcrop as big as a barn, a stone-built basin, and a fallen slab twelve feet by six, waist-high, out of the wind.
   329	- **The abstracts, read in daylight (flag).** Karis counted every row twice and had each counted again (Lira the first, Cael the second, Brom the third; the fourth set by for Seln; the sixth her own); **no figure for any row is printed**. **Five groups of signs, the same five in all six rows.** Four are the hide's four by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double). **The fifth is "larger again than the fourth"; not on the hide; its place unknown.** **Five columns:** the day; the station (eight points, by the compass and its halves); the measure; *Instruments*; and the anomaly. No radius of any place has changed by a stride in six years; four surveyors by their figures; three clerks' hands (round; cramped and leaning backward; capitals like gates). The fourth column reads *Cord true. Edge true. Level true.* on every sheet. The three oldest rows have a ruled space at the foot headed *Remarks*, empty but once: **the Stair's sheet in the fourth row, in a hand that Karis cannot tell from the dead surveyor's**, carries the protected remark, against the fifth column. From the next year's printing the space is gone.
   330	- **Seln's five omissions:** no routing head; no surveyor's name, a number in its place; nothing to say who ordered the survey; no column for a cause; the remarks space struck from the block after the year of the remark.
   331	- **Vastin on the form (flag).** "A survey abstract of the second schedule… the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it." Drawn at the year's end in the office that has the charge of the stations. **That office keeps its own year, which opens later than the seat's; a date in the stations' count must be turned by taking off the difference, a fixed number of days (given aloud to Karis; not printed). A return that comes up from a station is dated so; a paper that goes down from the seat is dated the other way.** His two false starts: an order to re-tape each row under two hands, and "restricted matter… under the first article of the closed schedule".
   332	- **The box (flag).** To the right of the four signs and the number, on every abstract, a small printed square with a heading in the unread signs. **On all four of the hide's places it holds the short stroke and the bar, drawn alike; on the fifth it is "not filled as ours are"** (Karis will draw it and describe it to nobody). "It is a class." Nobody says who filled it or by what rule.
   333	- **The sum (flag).** The registry writes, on the first line of a person's file, after the name and before the bracketed word, the day on which the person was classed, in the seat's count; every paper served on Cael began with it. Seln copied that line twice when he was employed at the house above Ostrand. The technician's date, turned, is that day. **No figure and no span is on the page.** Seln took the sheet from the foot of the second row during the reading and did the turn three ways at the basin; he set it before Cael with its head away from him.
   334	- **The fork-stone and the fold (flag).** A slab as tall as Brom, a worn hand cut on each face. A round drovers' pen of dry stone a little down the northern branch, a quarter of it roofed with slabs, with a hearth and flue in the wall and a stack of peat: "You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go." A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it is not on a road.
   335	- **Seln on the provision (flag):** "A provision of that kind is granted by the week." Belief in the first; work in the second; nobody leaves at the end of a second.
   336	- **Treswick from the rise.** A green plain with hedged fields; a river out of the north in loops; a bluff in the nearest loop; three walls in steps (a long grey one at the foot, a second with slate roofs inside it, a third close and pale about the crown); a small pale steady light above the highest roofs. A day's walk off.
   337	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the shoulder hold's factor and "the hold's woman"; the broker and his clerk on the western landing; the keeper of the house for the sick, the Gold with the wrist and the woman from the river sheds in Oryn's account.
   338	
   339	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   340	**Calendar.** A little before noon on the **two hundred and eighty-first day past the Line**, on the top of the long rise west of the fork-stone, a day's walk from Treswick. Days on the page: 278, 279, 280, 281. No season, month or weekday name; no figures for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   341	
   342	**Where everyone is.** Six going down the western side of the rise in file: Seln, Lira, Brom, Karis, Cael, and Vastin last. Oryn and the brown mule went north from the fork at first light and did not look back. Teague's five are east of the river.
   343	
   344	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments; no eleventh** (he waited for one beside the wall and nothing came). **Deployment thirty-two.** Left palm stung by Ember. He ruled on a job and has made no vow; nobody has voted on the town. **He has read the technician's date, turned it, and knows it for the day of his own Kindling. He has not written it, has told nobody, and has put no verb to it.** He has written in the log, at Oryn's asking, that inside Treswick he does not go near the station. He has laid his hand on the page. He has seen the light on the crown and turned from it. He carries a box on his back. He does not know Seln's reasons, only that the sheet was set before him without a word. He has entered his own plan at the cut as wrong.
   345	
   346	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Arm: the sixteenth day out of the wool on the 281st** (fifteen on the 280th, Oryn's count). It held a hound by the scruff while the right hand struck; it votes, swings a box to a shoulder, and carries the tarred case. Oryn: slow for as long again; don't fall on it. Ankle sound. **She no longer has the crossbow.** She knows that Cael has put something away and has not asked.
   347	
   348	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey at any point. Right shoulder still in the older healer's knot ("a month and more from her"; if it tells him anything he is to put the load down and leave it down). **Right foot: two toes bruised, bound; ten days from the 279th, eight of them left on the 281st.** One box on the left shoulder. He has the purse and Pike's thumbed slip. He lays seven bowls and will carry Oryn's empty.
   349	
   350	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; one point set in the peats. She holds the flat (six rows, counted and re-taped, the technician's sheet at the foot of the second), the completion report, the page in its oilcloth, her copy, the hide, the kit's ledger and her notebook. She knows: five groups; the fifth larger than the fourth and unplaced; five columns; no measure changed in six years; the instruments true; the remark and whose hand it looks like; the box, the same on four and different on the fifth; the stations' year and its difference. She cannot read the index or the box. She began a sentence about lamps and stopped it. She has asked nobody about the date; she heard "I've read it" from Cael as she had from Seln. She has said that she does not know whether she is more afraid of being right or wrong. Six findings on her stillhound leaf.
   351	
   352	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. One hound with a short knife. **He has done the sum three ways, has told nobody, and has set the sheet before Cael without a word; he does not know for certain that it was read.** He has taught for the first time ("Draft nothing. Just look.", to Vastin). One box on his back above the case's strap. **The case is on him, shut; no ruling upon it.** Oryn has told him to change shoulders; he will "consider it".
   353	
   354	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. On Pike's slip as its healer, of her own road. She asked Vastin and had an explicit yes; shut four holes; bound the joint; two fingers on his wrist. She has priced all six and given Vastin as long as any. **She has not read Cael** and has said so. She has told the city, the column and why she left. She has her term in his log and has read it there. Gone north with the mule, her linen, and her crossbow with its one bolt; five gates, the ford, then Millrace in three weeks. She has not heard the sum, the fifth place or the box as anything but talk from the basin.
   355	
   356	**Vastin.** No office; **no age stated**. **Iron Wall declared once, on the 279th, in the cut**; spent to the floor. **Right knee: bitten, shut, bound stiff; the 281st is the second day of thirty**; on the stick; no kneeling, running or walls. He qualified every instruction and has kept each to the letter. **He has not sat in front of the crew since the cut.** Hands in strips of linen; the left still does not open. He knows the four hands, the doctrine and where a post stands; he has been passed over by a hound. He has heard the daylight reading whole. **He does not know the sum** (he gave the difference as procedure and has never weighed the sheet's date), the mechanism, what Cael carries, the instruments' behaviour in Cael's hand, or what is under the bar; nothing has been said of a directive or an objection. He paid his own fare, the loft twice, and a copper at the hold; Brom would not take one for a bowl on the road. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He carries nothing but his pack.
   357	
   358	**Teague and his crew.** On the east bank; salt going over; then the cold road. He knows the six went west to the cut "and what's beyond it", and that Vastin walks behind them. His term is "where I left it". The tall man said nothing.
   359	
   360	**Pike's book.** Eleven completed lines. The clearance is taken (five strokes and a healer's line) and not yet said closed; **the thumbed slip is in Brom's shirt**. The season line's number still blank. Eleventh on the cloth.
   361	
   362	**Objects and money.** **A hundred and thirty-eight marks** (118 + 20 at the hold's gate). The reserve sealed. **Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman.** Three boxes on three backs (Brom, Seln, Cael). The kit's case with Lira. Stake thirty-two never driven. The cord and both edges untouched by Cael since the 168th. Hesk's book has the term on the 280th's leaf.
   363	
   364	**The Quieting, as known.** On paper: five places, measured for six years with no change; instruments true inside; one word in the fifth column; one surveyor's remark, and the space for remarks removed afterward; a class mark that is the same on four and different on the fifth. In the field: unchanged.
   365	
   366	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them). The daylight reading: the six at the slab, and Oryn by hearing. **The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone.** Nobody has interpreted the sequence, said who classed the places or why, or joined the remark to anything.
   367	
   368	**Words said aloud for the first time.** *Iron Wall* (Vastin, 16:107). The town's name, for the first time since the page, and *Cleon*, inside marker 4.
   369	
   370	**Open threads.** Six markers. A date that two people know and neither has said. "All of it." A fifth place with no bearing. A box that nobody can read. A lamps sentence stopped at its fourth word. A knee on its second day. A reading that a healer owes. Two sentences in a log about a flight of steps and a paved place. A rope of the wrong lay. A question that Cael kept from Vastin at Lowmarch, still kept.
   371	
   372	### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
   373	Movement 4 already carries day 281, six people/no mule, boxes on Brom/Lira/Cael, kit case on Vastin, the road posts, the inward reads, the station promise, and no new fragment (`packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:24,31–33,43–46`). Its author must additionally inherit the following surviving details:
   374	
   375	- **Paper custody:** Karis has the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; inside the flat are six counted/re-taped rows with the original technician's sheet restored to the end of row two, plus the completion report and her copy; Vastin's copied page remains in its oilcloth inside her coat; Cael laid his hand on it once, on the rise, and gave it back (coordinator ruling: the author's beat is kept) (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:141–149,155–157`; `manuscript/chapter-19.md:207,271–291`). Karis also has the dead surveyor's ledger/notebook; Seln's case remains shut on his own strap (`manuscript/chapter-17.md:183–187`; `manuscript/chapter-18.md:39–41`).
   376	- **Knowledge:** Cael and Seln alone know that the unprinted date matches Cael's Kindling day; neither has said or written it, neither has interpreted the sequence, and neither knows for certain what the other concluded (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:3–31,59–65,85–149`). Karis knows both men have read the sheet but not what they made of it (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:143–149`). Vastin heard the records reading but does not know the arithmetic, integration mechanism or Cael's fragments (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:159–205`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:266`). Oryn heard the archive discussion from the basin but not the date conclusion (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:229–231`; `packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:56`).
   377	- **Bodies:** Vastin's right knee is shut, lath-bound and on day two of Oryn's thirty-day month on the 281st; stick, no kneeling, no running, no wall, and he has finally accepted being priced equally (`manuscript/chapter-16.md:27–65`; `manuscript/chapter-19.md:151–169`). Brom's right two toes are bound with eight days left on the ten-day term, and his right shoulder remains in the older healer's knot; he must put a load down if it speaks (`manuscript/chapter-16.md:75`; `manuscript/chapter-19.md:131–139`). Lira's arm remains slow and is on day sixteen out of wool (`manuscript/chapter-19.md:119–129`).
   378	- **Oryn:** she and the brown mule are north of the fork on the reverse circuit; she has her linen and crossbow with one bolt. Her stated route is five gates, the ford and Millrace, where she expects the six in three weeks; she still owes Cael the proper reading and expects “all of it” from the archive (`manuscript/chapter-19.md:63–81,171–219`).
   379	- **Money/objects:** purse 138; reserve sealed; sixty yards of four-strand still owed; Pike's thumbed slip is in Brom's shirt; no cordage was bought (`manuscript/chapter-13.md:149–165`; `manuscript/chapter-16.md:99–101,187`). Oryn's empty bowl is Brom's to carry (`manuscript/chapter-19.md:221–223`).
   380	- **Progression:** ten fragments, deployment THIRTY-TWO (coordinator ruling: one Ember grain at the mark and one Wind burst away, both at the cut); Shadow sealed, Anchor still two, Tide unused, no self-read, no eleventh (`manuscript/chapter-15.md:133–163,223–227`; `manuscript/chapter-16.md:205–211`). Vastin has shown Iron Wall once; Cael witnessed it but did not acquire it (`packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:55`; `packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:52`).
   381	- **Treswick/day:** it is a little before noon on day 281, at the top of the long rise, a day's walk from Treswick. The six have seen a bluff, river loops, three tiered walls and the pale steady station light; Cael turned his eyes to the lowest wall, and Lira has not yet performed the packet's unasked matching discipline (`manuscript/chapter-19.md:233–269`; `packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:24,43`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Three days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:33:"He has done nothing that I could see to be different." Karis had come down the yard, and was frowning at the slip. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it. Do it again. I shall watch the hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:109:"No," said Karis. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:227:"And then I find out whether I've been right for five years or wrong for five years, and I don't know which I'm more afraid of."

