editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the registry's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:65:The fifth column she had read by a lamp already. There were three clerks' hands in six years: a round one, and a cramped one that leaned backward, and one that made its capitals like gates. Each of them had written the same single word against every station of every place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write. He was a man who lived among instruments, whoever he was, and he knew what a device does. It does the same thing at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule." She had laid her pencil across the sheet's foot, to keep the wind from its corner. "That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:123:"I have five things that are not there," he said. "I shall give them in the order of their weight, which is the reverse of the order in which a person notices them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:131:"The fifth is yours. You found the remark; I looked for what came after it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:"On the form that those four words were written upon, there is a space at the foot for remarks. On the next year's printing there is none. The rule that closed it off has been taken out of the block, and the fifth column runs to the foot of the sheet." His mild voice did not alter. "Somebody read them. It is the only sign in six rows that anybody read anything at all; and what he did, having read them, was to see that no surveyor should have room to write four more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:5:It took her three breaths, in which Cael saw her eyes go to five grey shapes, and leave them, since they were past her trade. They went to a girl who was wiping a long knife on a tuft of bents with her left hand; to a small man who had come out of the broken ice wet to the shins and was looking at nothing; to a large one sitting on a stone with his foot out. They came to Cael, and passed over him as they passed over a gatepost. Then they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:17:Cael watched him look for the form of it. A man who had spent his life granting leave, and refusing it, under his hand and a seal, was being asked a plain thing by a woman on a road who had no office to ask it from. He looked at her bare forearms. He looked, for an instant, at the five who stood about him, and it came to Cael that he was waiting to hear whether any of them would answer for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:83:"Five," said the factor, at Brom's stone. "I said five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:87:The crook was leaned against the bank. The factor climbed the low side with the ease of a person who has done it since childhood, and stood on the top with both hands in the apron's belt, looking down fifty-five yards of scree.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:"Empty's what I posted. I never asked anybody for safe. I've lived on this shoulder since I was born, and that seep'll have its glass back by morning." A hand went into the apron. "There's another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. They'll have to learn it from the beginning, as these did; and by then I'll have had thirty carts of salt up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:The twenty was counted out on the top bar of the gate, in four heaps of five, and a thumb was set to the foot of Pike's slip, where it left a mark like a crook. Cael had been paid at a good many gates by then, and he had learned to read a hold by how it paid. The factor at the axe-head had counted twice, like a man who has been cheated before; this one counted once, and did not look at the heaps afterward, like a person who has got exactly what was wanted and means to think no more about the price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:109:"It is called Iron Wall," said Vastin. "It is on a roll, with a rank beside it which would mean nothing on this road. I had not declared it in a great many years. I should be obliged if a carter were told that it was declared by a fool, in the wrong place, and that he was got out of it by five people who never made a sound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:119:"That a plane stood across a cut, from a line that cracked the ice. That it stood for as long as he did not move, and went when he let it. That eight struck it and none passed." Her pencil went on. "And I have added a sixth line to my five. *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* I had the three wrecks under my eyes from the foot for the better part of an hour, and I did not read them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:The seep had been at work in the night, and where a heap of hounds had broken the glass there was a new skin already, clear as a window, with the crack still showing white beneath it in a line from bank to bank. The factor's people had dragged the five away, and nothing else in that place was any different. Cael walked past Brom's stone, and past the spot where a stick had come down, and did not put his boot on the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:71:"North by that rut at first light. There are five gates up along the hill-foot that I've not seen since before the snow, and one of them has a child in it that I was to have heard a cold term ago. Then down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above. It's my own round, walked the wrong way about. The mill has waited all the cold for me, and it'll wait as long again and say that I'm late." Her eyes went to the small pale woman on the meal-sack. "How long does a person want, to read a hole under a town?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:81:"Three weeks is five gates and a ford, at my pace, with a day in hand," said Oryn. "That'll put me at Millrace. I'll be at the mill's bench when you come down off that road, and if you're late I'll hear of it from a carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:109:*The fork. Oryn north; five holds; routing, not the other verb. Her term, given at the fire, taken in the log because that's the form she'll take it in: inside Treswick I do not go near the station. Not the steps. Not the square. Written here so she can read it, and so I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:19:It went out to the left and stayed there. Cael looked where it went, and for the space of several strides he saw only the mist and the heather; then a piece of the mist moved against the rest, and he had the shape of it. It was low, and long in the back, and it carried its head stretched out level. It was going along the slope at a walk, forty-five yards off, at exactly the pace of a mule, and not one sprig of heather stirred round its legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:67:"Pike's five, for the cut. I asked for five, and I see five and two over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:81:"You know what took them, or Pike's sent me the wrong five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:95:"There's a hole below the road on the north side, where that water goes into the hill. They lie up round it. I've seen five. I'd not swear to five, and nobody who's been nearer has come back to tell me better."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:115:"Fifty-five yards," said Seln, under his breath. "From the road's edge to the water. I have counted it three times against that thorn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:"Five," said Seln. "Under the thorn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:125:They were grey on grey, and they did not move, and it took Cael the better part of a quarter-hour to be certain of the fifth. They lay as dogs lie that have nothing to wait for, each in its own scrape, with its nose toward the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:127:"Five is what can be seen from here," said Seln. "There is a strip under the north bank that this lip hides, perhaps four yards of it, the whole length of the cut. I cannot tell you what lies in it. I should not care to say five to a factor who has just told me five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:129:"Then we'll say five that we've seen," said Cael, "and plan for more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:135:"There." Lira did not point. "And there, and a third at the top. See the bank? It's worn. Three places, all in the upper half. The stone's a different colour where they've gone over it." She was silent for a little, studying the scree under her own chin. "This side's bad going. It's all plates. If I ran on that, it'd run with me. I could cross it slow, on my hands, or I could go down it sitting. I'd not care to be a thing with four legs trying to come up it in a hurry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"I shall read you five lines that you have heard, and then I shall say what I think they are worth here," she said. "It hunts what a Path gives off. It hears, and sees a little, and kills on neither. Water masks it. It has no leader, and where nothing moves first, it stands. And it learns." She laid her pencil on the last. "After three had been killed at a ford by people who had declared nothing, the others would not come near such people. I have that from one evening, at one river, and I shall not pretend that it is more. It is all that we have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:171:"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:233:*Yes*, he told the fifth of the ten, for the first time in earnest since a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:25:Five of them read it together from the left-hand post, since Lira and Brom had come round from the well, and Seln had been standing there already, with no wand under his arm. It was a strip of bark, like every other slip on that oak, in the square black hand. Beside the words Pike had drawn a little road going between two heaps, and on the road something with four legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:33:Cael read the slip a second time, as he read anything, and it was the last five words that he read. Two roads left the western landing. One ran away to the south and west for three hundred miles, to a ford and a station and a house with a seal, and an old man had come down it on a stick. The other was the border road. It went up over a moor to the Line by the short way, and Cael knew what stood at the end of it, since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and told him; and last night he had read it again, in a square black copy, under a title.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:79:"Five," said Pike. He made the strokes at the slip's foot, and then he did not dip his pen again. He turned his shoulder to them. "And who's your healer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:85:Nobody at the post said the name. They had laid a sixth bowl for it every night since a heap of stones, and had seen it eaten out of standing; and not one of the five would have named her to a ledger without her leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:"I've a bench here that's done by noon, and it's had six mornings of me. The mill's my second stop. It's waited a whole cold term, and it can wait four days more while I go round by the top and come down on it from above; I've five gates up that way that I'd have had to climb to in any case." She threw the cloth over her shoulder. "I said once that somebody ought to stand at an edge with her sleeves up. I'm not having a girl with a fortnight's arm go into a cut behind something that takes carts, and hearing about it afterward from a carter. Write me. I'm a line on that slip. I'm not a name under theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:95:"That's posted," he said slowly. "A clearance wants a healer. It's my own rule, and you've answered it, and I can write posted." The pen went into the horn. Beneath the five strokes he made a sixth line, apart from them, and said it over twice to the mist. "*And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.* That's the first time I've had a column for you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:103:It took that long to leave a place where five people had lain for a hundred and forty-eight nights. Brom carried the three boxes down from the hearth's end one at a time, roped as he had roped them in the night, and slung them on the brown mule with the beckets outward, while she stood for it at the shed's door with her ears back, like a person being measured for a coat. Karis walked beside the load with the hide under one arm and the flat under the other, and would give neither of them to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:The chin came up by the width of a finger. Then he turned to his sacks. The tall man went by with another on his shoulder, and gave five people and a healer the length of a stride, and not a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*

