editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:193:It let them through together, without any great noise. There was a long soft tearing, and a slab went down on one side and came up on the other, turning; and after that there was a piece of black water the shape of a slate, with a little grey ice rocking on it. He watched it for as long as he could have held his breath. Somewhere under the lip of rock the water went on going down into the hill, as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:189:The others were down already in the hay, where he could hear Brom turning his foot about to find a way that it would lie. He began as he began every entry, with the day, and with what could be counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:213:"Twenty-eight," said Oryn, without turning round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:71:"He is required to say how his instruments behaved. I suppose that somebody once wondered whether a cord, or a level, would go wrong in such a place as a sigil does. So there is a column. And in it, against every station, for six years, in four hands, there is one entry." She read it out. "*Cord true. Edge true. Level true.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:23:"That came over in the bag," he said, without turning. "I had it yesterday, with eleven pieces to give out and a boat that nearly went down to the mill. I don't pin by lamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:He had lifted papers out of heaps under their owners' eyes for the better part of his working life. One did not snatch. One was seen to be handling the heap for an honest reason, as he had been, reading six rows for what they left out. One's left hand squared the sheets while the right went on turning them. The sheet was in his sleeve before the pebble was back on its corner. Nobody at the slab had looked up. They were the only six people living from whom he had never before taken anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:53:The kindest thing that he knew how to do for the boy was, in every particular that could be seen from outside, the same act as the cruellest thing that he had been trained to do to a stranger. He could find no way round it. The difference lay wholly in what he wanted. He had been taught, by people who knew their business, that what a man wanted could not be seen and ought never to be trusted.
